1
00:00:00,450 --> 00:00:04,910
Around 540 BCE, a Babylonian
king dug up a ruin,

2
00:00:04,960 --> 00:00:07,580
and said it was thirty-two
hundred years old.

3
00:00:07,800 --> 00:00:12,460
He was Nabonidus, Babylon's last
king. Rebuilding a temple at Sippar,

4
00:00:12,510 --> 00:00:16,519
he dug down to an inscription
of Naram-Sin, a king of Akkad.

5
00:00:16,819 --> 00:00:21,809
Put them on one line. Separate related,
and the two kings rise into a track of

6
00:00:21,859 --> 00:00:27,809
their own. Naram-Sin ruled about 2250
BCE. The gap isn't thirty-two hundred

7
00:00:27,859 --> 00:00:30,563
years. It's about seventeen hundred.

8
00:00:30,823 --> 00:00:36,463
His number would put it before 3700 BCE,
earlier than any writing we know of.

9
00:00:36,513 --> 00:00:41,117
He was out by fifteen centuries, and he's
still called the first archaeologist.

10
00:00:41,437 --> 00:00:44,337
Every date on one line,
with where it comes from.

11
00:00:44,387 --> 00:00:46,916
That's OpenChronology, and it's free.
